#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088602} :activity/id #uuid "fd6a29fe-5312-5d49-b1ae-79d4178dc9eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048459 :topic/id #uuid "a163637a-ed19-547a-85e8-0acfdb20fa0f" :topic/math-academy-id 865} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088928 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x+1.\n$$\n\nThis function $f$ takes a real number $x$ as input, and it outputs a real number. We can describe this fact using the following notation:\n\n$$\nf: x \\longrightarrow 2x+1\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can define a function $\\mathbf T,$ also called a **transformation** or a **map**, that takes a 2D-vector (or 3D-vector) as input and outputs a new 2D-vector (or, respectively, 3D-vector). \n\nFor example, let's consider the following transformation: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis means that for any 2D-vector $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ the corresponding image $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this transformation just like we would with any other function. For example, applying the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ to the input vector \n$[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+3 \\\\ 2\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe say that the transformation $\\mathbf T$ *maps* the input vector $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ to the output vector $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58dc3c80-54e0-549f-afc1-e5a3b8cbea52" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3525 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b716871b-bb81-5bf2-8223-80c9510b7921" :step/math-academy-id 19995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087562 :question/id #uuid "07d823d0-39f8-5d70-8df4-7fd15783e148" :question/math-academy-id "e-5427"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050802}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396176}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "15549c3e-d1ae-52a9-b57f-520764e652b6" :step/math-academy-id 19996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087563 :question/id #uuid "8fe1f455-e739-59cb-95c2-0378dff32b8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5428"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393431}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"} :step/id #uuid "c11e935e-6e42-5053-8e60-e60d238b8a80" :step/math-academy-id 19997 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087564 :question/id #uuid "e34b53de-72d8-541f-bd04-16d2cbf1e6b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5429"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "381f7ffe-ae4b-5e45-9fa9-e2a6bd8348b3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393438}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "b01168ef-ba36-595a-90db-36c9c6fc6b90" :step/math-academy-id 19998 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087565 :question/id #uuid "fc3159fb-751c-55d6-b41e-4744b80b0ae9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5430"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049537}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398996}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "8f470406-9e89-58e2-bd83-097c8b921250" :step/math-academy-id 19999 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088603}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088940 :tutorial/content "A transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is called a **linear transformation** (or **linear map**) if the following two properties are satisfied:\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and any scalar $k$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1+\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)+\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_2)$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$\n\nIf we let $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then the conditions above can be written as follows:\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\n\nWe can determine whether the transformation $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]$ is linear by checking if these two conditions are true.\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)+(ky) \\\\ 2(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ k(2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth conditions are satisfied, so this transformation is linear." :tutorial/id #uuid "5951013b-06e0-5923-bbd4-d8440d67679e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3935 :tutorial/title "Linear Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "e84e1380-e6a4-5d10-9efe-3491fa692281" :step/math-academy-id 23253 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088604}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087932}]}
